+## Key and password files
+
+One rule reads every key or password file: the HubSpot key (`DESK_HUBSPOT_KEY_FILE`), the live-model key (`DESK_LIVE_KEY_FILE`), the SMTP password (`DESK_SMTPP_PASSWORD_FILE`), the mailbox password (`REAL_IMAP_n_PASSWORD_FILE`), the n8n API key of `scripts/n8n_api.py` (`N8N_API_KEY_FILE`) and the key of the demo model server. It lives in one function (`read_secret_file` in `src/lead_desk/envnum.py`); nothing else in the code opens such a file. Every refusal text names the setting and never shows the path or the key.
+
+* What the file holds: one line of at least 8 printable ASCII characters. The file may be at most 4096 bytes, counting the line break, and is kept as text (UTF-8). Only the line break at the very end of the file is removed; a blank at the start, in the middle or at the end (a plain space, a tab, a no-break space) is refused. The one exception is the SMTP password, whose plain spaces the desk drops wherever they stand (a tab, a no-break space or any other blank is still refused). The mailbox password file follows the SMTP password rule: spaces inside are dropped. A hidden first character that some editors add (a byte-order mark) is accepted and removed. Three faults are told apart, each with its own fixed text: empty or too short, a character that is not printable ASCII, and a space inside a key. A file of several lines, or one that is not UTF-8 text, is refused too.
+* What kind of file: a regular file. A folder, a named pipe, a device or a symbolic link is refused without being read. A link in a folder above the file is followed; the file itself must be the real file, because a link can be pointed at another file after you checked it. The desk opens the file without following a link and then looks again at the name: a link, or a name that now points at another file, is refused ("the file changed while it was being opened: try again").
+* Who may read it (Linux and macOS): a file that other users can read or write is refused with "the file can be read by other users: run chmod 600 on it". There is no way to switch this off, and the owner of the file is not checked. Create the file with `chmod 600` (or start the shell with `umask 077` first). The owner is not looked at because the mode alone says who else can read it, and an owner check would refuse a key kept in a shared service account. Windows has no matching mode bits, so nothing is checked there; keep the file in a folder only you can open (not tried by the author).
+* When the file is read: the HubSpot key, the SMTP password and the live-model key are each read once at the start of the real service that uses them (so a bad file stops the start, exit code 4). The live-model key is read again at its first call and kept in memory only after that (a file replaced before the first call is the one used). The CRM key (`crm_hubspot` calls `_key()` for each call) and the mail password are read again at every call. The mailbox password is not read at start; it is read each time the mailbox is counted. The key is not kept from the start-up read, so each of these files must stay in place and readable for as long as the desk runs; "read once at the start" does not mean "kept".
+* Whether the key is valid at the vendor cannot be checked without a network call.
 
